from pathlib import Path
import json,re
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
for c in json.loads((B/'courses.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8')):
 root=B/'stage'/str(c['stage']);test=(root/c['test']).read_text(encoding='utf-8');names=re.findall(r'(?:test|it)\([\'\"]([^\'\"]+)',test)
 lines=['# Trace lab: '+c['title'],'','[Course index](README.md) · [Worked change](03-WORKED-CHANGE.md) · [Tests](04-TESTING-AND-DEBUGGING.md)','','## The concrete case','','This lab connects one visible action to the code that accepts or rejects it. Use a disposable dataset and write your prediction before running the operation. The goal is to explain what each boundary contributes, not to memorize a sequence of clicks.','',c['example'],'','## Write the record before touching the form','',c['model'],'','Make a small table in your own notes with these columns: field, starting value, proposed value, and source of truth. Include identity even when the form does not display it. Include related rows or stored counters when the operation changes more than one fact. Mark a value as derived if the app recomputes it rather than storing it independently.','','For example, an edit can change a title while preserving an ID. A status command can keep the same status while producing no new history. An import can contain many rows yet be rejected before any replacement begins. In each case, the meaningful observation is the promised resulting state. A success toast alone cannot establish which fields changed.','','## Trace the real call path','']
 for i,flow in enumerate(c['flow'],1):lines+=['### Boundary '+str(i), '',flow+'.','',f'Find the relevant call in the source for this project, starting with [{c["source"]}](../{c["source"]}) and `{c["symbol"]}`. Record the actual argument names and the result or side effect. At this step, ask whether the value has been validated already or is merely typed by the compiler. Write down what a rejection would prevent and which later steps would not run.','']
 lines+=['## Predict the regression scenarios','', '| Scenario from the executable suite | Prediction to write before running |','|---|---|']
 for name in names:lines+=[f'| {name.replace("|","/")} | Initial records, request/input, expected result, and records that must remain unchanged. |']
 lines+=['','Open the test file and identify the arrange, act and assert portions. Arrange creates the synthetic starting state. Act calls the real boundary. Assert reads the observable result. Some tests repeat a call or issue two calls together; explain why a single ordinary call would miss that defect. Tests may share a deliberate fixture within one suite, so read setup and earlier scenario state before copying one case into a standalone script.','','## A successful request, described precisely','','Start with a valid fixture from the test. Copy the values into your own learning notes rather than copying a personal record. State which fields are required, which may be omitted and which have a special meaning when empty. Execute the operation once. Compare the response with a fresh authoritative read, then compare the UI with that read. If the UI is still loading, wait for the actual expected condition instead of guessing a fixed delay.','','Record the stable ID and one changed field. Record at least one field that must remain the same. If the operation creates a child, record the parent ID stored on the child. If it updates an aggregate, compute the expected aggregate by hand. If it restores a backup, count retained, ignored and recreated records according to this project’s documented policy. These observations make a small example reusable when the implementation changes.','','## A rejected request, described precisely','','Now change one value to a rejected case named in the test suite. Keep everything else identical. Predict where validation fails. Run it and capture both the feedback and the authoritative state afterward. Compare the saved record, related rows and relevant counters with their pre-request values. A controlled error is useful only if the application also preserves the intended state.','','Do not assume that all failures have the same status or message. Invalid input, missing records, conflicts and infrastructure errors represent different conditions. Read this app’s handler or parser to see the actual result. Browser-only parsers return a success/error union or throw an import error rather than returning an HTTP status. Translate the same correctness idea into the contract this app actually exposes.','','## Read the storage boundary','',c['dbguide'],'','Place a breakpoint immediately before the first write and another after the final write or publish step. On the success path, note what changed between those points. On the rejected-input path, the first write should never be reached when validation promises pre-write rejection. On a transactional failure path, some statements may execute and then roll back; reaching a write statement does not mean its effect committed.','','An await marks a place where another operation can run. It does not itself define a transaction. When the test uses Promise.all, sketch request A and request B in two columns. Show where each acquires a lock or reads state. The test should accept either winner while insisting on the same final invariant. For synchronous or browser-only validation, explain why concurrency is outside the tested function rather than borrowing a database explanation that does not apply.','','## Explain the design tradeoff','',c['concept'],'','## Change one requirement independently','', '**Practice task:** '+c['exercises'][0],'','Before implementation, write three acceptance criteria: an ordinary successful case, a rejected or cancelled case, and a case that preserves existing behavior. Identify the smallest file boundary that owns the rule. Keep the existing regression suite; add a test only when it observes a new promise. Do not reproduce the implementation line by line inside the assertion, because both copies can contain the same mistake.','','After a first attempt, compare your design with this hint: '+c['answers'][0],'','## Debug a disagreement between layers','','If the form and response disagree, inspect the request payload before editing the database code. If the response and database disagree, inspect the mapper, transaction and read query. If a test fails before an HTTP request is logged, inspect startup, environment variables and the test fixture. Missing dependencies, a busy port or an unavailable database are different from a failed business assertion. Preserve the first useful error and fix that cause before interpreting later cascading failures.','','When a date looks wrong, distinguish a calendar label from an instant and check the serialization boundary. When an optional field will not clear, distinguish omission from an explicit empty/null value. When a list seems to lose a record, inspect filtering, sorting and pagination before assuming deletion. When history duplicates, compare desired-state commands with commands that represent a new event. These are diagnostic questions to apply to the actual fields above.','','## Final independent checkpoint','','Close this guide and give a five-minute walkthrough using only the application source and one test. Explain the user-visible problem, the input contract, the identity model, the write boundary, a failed operation and the remaining limitation. Then modify the worked fixture and predict the result without running it. Reopen the guide only after writing your answer.','','A useful review note includes the exact command run, its result, and the boundary it covers. Keep browser interaction, simulated storage tests, real HTTP/database tests and compile checks distinct. The [verification page](VERIFICATION.md) records this delivery’s evidence. Exercises and proposed extensions in this course remain learner work; they are not counted as implemented features.','']
 (root/'astraupskill/07-TRACE-LAB.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines),encoding='utf-8')
print('Created 25 detailed trace labs')
